{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ centered about the point $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}\\,+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1. \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1+0\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{24}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)\\,\\,\\,\\, & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =0+(-2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(0)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+\\frac{(8)}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-126840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{8}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{2}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-\\frac{8}{3}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ about $x=1.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-4}}\n$(x-1)^{3}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =1+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-13911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${e}^{-{x}^{3}}$ about $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-13911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{3}}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ f(-1) & ={e}^{-(-1)^{3}}=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-3(-1)^{2}{e}^{-(-1)^{3}}=-3e \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-3{x}^{2})\\cdot (-3{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{-{x}^{3}}+(-6x)\\cdot {e}^{-{x}^{3}}=(9{x}^{4}-6x){e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =(9\\cdot (-1)^{4}-6\\cdot (-1)){e}^{-(-1)^{3}}=15e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} & =e+(-3e)(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =e-3e(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-13911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-13911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-13911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{e}+\\frac{2}{e}(x+1)+\\frac{16}{3e}(x+1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{e}-\\frac{3}{e}(x+1)+\\frac{15}{2e}(x+1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e+2e(x+1)+\\frac{4e}{3}(x+1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e-3e(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e-3e(x+1)-\\frac{16e}{3}(x+1)^{2}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-13911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e-3e(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.095"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =(0)+(-1)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(0)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(1)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=3$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3 & ≈-(3-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(3-\\pi )^{3} \\\\  & =0.141\\,12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-126874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,05"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,12"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,19"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,24"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,37"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126874" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,12"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-83201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-83201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(x-\\pi )^{4}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +1$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1) & ≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =-0.542,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-83201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-83201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-83201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.486"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.492"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.505"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.542"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.601"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-83201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.542"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{1/3}$ about $x=8$ to approximate the value of ${7}^{1/3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=8.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/3} \\\\ f(8) & ={8}^{1/3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/3} & =2+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}(x-8)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-8}{12}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ into the above to approximate ${7}^{1/3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{1/3} & ≈2+\\frac{7-8}{12} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{25}{12}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡4.$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\pi ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(0)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=4$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4 & ≈-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(4-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(4-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(4-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-0.654\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2"} {"question" "q-126878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.643"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.649"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.654"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.658"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.662"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "q-126878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.654"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f222c3e626d1532c011dc871696e604faf25c8f4a183082c04a741286c12838"} {"question" "e-752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-752.json" "file_sha256" "d846b6a3647cbc0b9c6e99c3d0c178ef79e23dc01eb271adba4f1b97e9e6ded7"} {"question" "e-752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-752.json" "file_sha256" "d846b6a3647cbc0b9c6e99c3d0c178ef79e23dc01eb271adba4f1b97e9e6ded7"} {"question" "e-753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-753.json" "file_sha256" "0521b0c38b33d927ec7e4dbd7bec13df611a9d990e01a5228460198bfa630fdb"} {"question" "e-753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.1} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(9.1-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(9.1-9)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(0.1)-\\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.016\\,620,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places. \n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-753.json" "file_sha256" "0521b0c38b33d927ec7e4dbd7bec13df611a9d990e01a5228460198bfa630fdb"}] "usage" {"e-752" [] "e-753" []} "basis" 1483 "retractions" [[17592186184033 :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3$."] [17592186184033 :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nand $a=3$.\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= e^{-x} \\\\\nf(3) &= e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= -e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{′}(3) &= -e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{″}(3) &= e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= -e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{‴}(3) &= -e^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-x} &= e^{-3} + (-e^{-3})(x - 3) + ((e^{-3}))/(2!)(x - 3)^{2} + ((-e^{-3}))/(3!)(x - 3)^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= e^{-3} - e^{-3}(x - 3) + \\frac{e^{-3}}{2}(x - 3)^{2} - \\frac{e^{-3}}{6}(x - 3)^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186184034 :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$."] [17592186184034 :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots\n$$\n\nwith $a=9$.\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{1/2} \\\\\nf(9) &= 3 \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\nf^{′}(9) &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= -\\frac{1}{4}x^{-3/2} \\\\\nf^{″}(9) &= -\\frac{1}{108}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x} &= 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(x - 9) + \\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x - 9)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(x - 9) - \\frac{1}{216}(x - 9)^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}$. We get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{9.1} \\mid \\approx 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(9.1 - 9) - \\frac{1}{216}(9.1 - 9)^{2}; \\approx 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(0.1) - \\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2}; = 3.016620\n$$\n\nto six decimal places.\n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
